A||||25||lowCG||Buy a swaybar lately?||||1||
Z||000000||lowCG||06-27-2000||10:09 PM||||I've found that the Fierostore,JCWhitney are fresh out of the Addco supplied swaybars,seems that they're on perpetual backorder.<BR>Does anyone know of an alternative source for rear swaybars?<BR>I'd like to find an Addco one,or one that mounts in the same place.<BR>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||06-28-2000||10:19 AM||||Unless you plan on racing, just find a used 7/8" bar made for the front.  You can mount that on the rear.  Gotta drill a couple holes, but it's not supposed to be a big deal.  There are instructions out there somewhere.  If I can find them, I'll post.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ratt||06-28-2000||11:22 AM||||I did that mod to my Fiero & my Wife's fiero.  Just do a search for ratt postings & you'll find the instructions on how to do it.  The one thing I forgot at the time was that I used 2 poly bushings back to back between the swaybar & the control arm (not 1 as I stated).  You can also go to <A HREF="http://fierozone.tripod.com/weekend/swaybar/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fierozone.tripod.com/weekend/swaybar/index.html</A>   He also did this & has pics (my endlink has no space between the bushings which results in more ground clearance than what he did).||||reg||6
Z||000003||cifc||06-28-2000||12:41 PM||||I bought one recently from carparts.com.  It took about 3 weeks for it to be delivered though.  It's an addco bar.  I forget what I paid, but I had a coupon for 33% off.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||06-28-2000||04:22 PM||||I bought my rear sway bar a couple months ago from the Fiero Store (don't know what brand), but I guess they've ran out of stock now. JC Whitney was cheaper, but they were out of stock too. They told me Addco recently moved to a new facility and was having trouble catching up with their backlog, which apparently was bad even before they moved. Sorry LowCG, you may have to look elsewhere if you're in a hurry. I've saved Ratt's article about installing a front bar onto the rear, if anyone wants it.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 06-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||06-28-2000||04:56 PM||||Thanks,that's a good article,probably would've done that if I had an extra bar lying around.<BR>My phone calls have turned up an offer I can't refuse,however,an individual who works for a swaybar manufacturer has offered to R&D one on my car,will cost me the same as a Fierostore unit,will be made to my specs(attaches further outboard on the control arms than the Addco bar),and will include hiem ends for the outside connections,urethane for the inner.<BR>I should have it by the end of next week after I return from a trip.<p>[This message has been edited by lowCG (edited 06-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||06-28-2000||05:56 PM||||What's a "heim end"???||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||06-28-2000||09:07 PM||||This might be too small to see, but on these traction bars, there are 2 heim ends on each one of the bars, they are attached to both ends of the smaller rods. Racers like 'em.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jegs.com/images/products/ssbars_small.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 06-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Spyder||06-28-2000||11:43 PM||||I found out something neat about my sway bars from Addco with poly bushings. Since I’ve lowered the front (more than I should) of my car the sway bars ended up acting like springs keeping the car from lowering. I had to buy some new bolts and cut down the spacers.<P>Please note:<BR>This car has been lowered beyond conventional lowering.<BR>So it maybe something to check on a normal lowered Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||06-29-2000||09:29 AM||||bb-- What's the purpose on a sway bar?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Formula||06-29-2000||10:35 AM||||If you dont have a sway bar then when you turn the car it sways..........I think?||||reg||1
Z||000011||batboy||06-29-2000||02:23 PM||||Formula, I think Monkey is asking why use that heim end on the sway bar. I'm not sure exactly what LowCG is cooking up, but the heim will allow a limited range of movement without any sloppiness like rubber end links would. More precise handling is what he's after, plus they're very strong. I think heim joints and ends were originally used in the aircraft industry (I could be wrong).||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||06-29-2000||03:24 PM||||So would the ends of his sway bar be solidly mounted or would they still be attached by a bushing?  I guess I'm still confused.  Thanx, but nevermind.  Too stupid for highly technical discussions like this one. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
